---
/**
 * Redesign option — Flows → Journeys → Playbooks case study.
 *
 * Reframes the live /work/beyond-the-task.astro (currently a heavily
 * placeholder-flagged page — 9+ "[ADD: ...]" evidence markers, no real
 * metrics, explicit "to be verified" language on AI Playbooks status) into
 * the brief's product-evolution story. The product decision described here
 * (AI proposes, admin decides) is the one piece of real content in the
 * source page; everything else that was a placeholder there is marked
 * ContentNeeded here too, rather than invented fresh.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseHero from "../../components/CaseHero.astro";
import DecisionBlock from "../../components/DecisionBlock.astro";
import ConfidentialityNotice from "../../components/ConfidentialityNotice.astro";
import SignalChain from "../../components/SignalChain.astro";
import FlowsMorph from "../../components/FlowsMorph.astro";
import ScreenGallery from "../../components/ScreenGallery.astro";
import FilmPlayer from "../../components/FilmPlayer.astro";
import SectionLabel from "../../components/SectionLabel.astro";
const J = "/images/work/journeys";
const FL = "/images/work/flows";
---

<Base title="Flows → Journeys → Playbooks | Kishore Sudhamalla" description="A product evolution story: Flows shipped, Journeys shipped and in use by clients, and Signals & Playbooks, the AI-native layer, still in build.">
  <CaseHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · Darwinbox"
    title="From Flows to Journeys to Playbooks."
    lede="Rethinking employee experiences for an AI-native HCM platform. A product evolution story rather than a single redesign. Journeys is released and in use by clients. AI Playbooks is still in build and its scope may continue to change."
    meta={[
      { k: "Role", v: "Senior Manager, UX Design" },
      { k: "Scope", v: "Product strategy through craft, stakeholder communication, leadership buy-in" },
      { k: "Status", v: "Journeys shipped · Playbooks in build" },
      { k: "Platform", v: "Darwinbox HCM" },
    ]}
    img={`${J}/journeys-hero.webp`}
    alt="My Journeys · Functional Training"
    cards={[
      { eyebrow: "What it replaced", value: "Guided", label: "One Journey, not scattered Flows", note: "Every step an employee needs, grouped and tracked in one place instead of disconnected requests." },
      { eyebrow: "Creation framework", value: "3 paths", label: "Template, prompt, or from scratch", note: "What an admin sees instead of a blank page. Other Darwinbox modules later picked up the same pattern." },
    ]}
  />

  <ConfidentialityNotice note="Flows and Journeys are released Darwinbox products. AI Playbooks is in build and confidential, so the screens shown for it further down are an early concept exploration, not the real or final interface, and its scope may continue to change after my time on the project. This case study focuses on product strategy, design decisions and publicly communicable direction." />

  <article class="container-pg r-case">
    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="context">Context</SectionLabel>
      <h2>Flows already existed. The real gap was the experience around them.</h2>
      <p>
        Darwinbox had Flows, which handled standard tasks like approvals, notifications and data updates. They worked fine individually, but they were completely isolated. An employee going through an offboarding or a transfer hit a series of disconnected steps, with nothing connecting them into a single clear experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        There was also a hidden operational cost. Setting up a flow usually meant having an implementation or Customer Success rep sit with the admin, and every flow was built entirely from scratch. That made flow creation a major bottleneck.
      </p>
      <p>
        On top of that, a guided experience has to serve two very different people at once. Admins need deep flexibility to handle complex company rules, but employees should never see that complexity, they just need a clear next step. Adding AI only raises the stakes: the system has to know when it is confident enough to suggest something, while the admin keeps final control to review and override.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem framing -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="judgment">Problem framing</SectionLabel>
      <h2>The question changed twice.</h2>
      <p>
        First, we solved how to make guided experiences useful across different workflows. That became Journeys: a guided path for employees, configured by admins on top of existing Flows.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once Journeys launched, we noticed a new problem: admins still had to build every Journey from scratch, even though the system already had the employee data and patterns to suggest one. The question shifted.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="r-pullquote">From "how do we help an admin build a better Journey?" to "why should they have to build it manually at all?"</blockquote>
      <p>That second question is what Playbooks solves.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Creation framework -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="craft">Framework</SectionLabel>
      <h2>Replacing blank pages with a smart creation schema.</h2>
      <p>
        Before this, clicking "Create Journey" meant building everything manually, field by field. We fixed that by splitting the initial blank page into three choices:
      </p>

      <div class="r-schema-grid">
        <ul class="r-schema-list">
          <li><strong>Predefined templates:</strong> battle-tested Darwinbox defaults admins can use right away.</li>
          <li><strong>Prompt-driven creation:</strong> describing what you need in plain English and letting the system spin up a starting structure.</li>
          <li><strong>From scratch:</strong> the old manual builder, kept as a fallback instead of the default.</li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="r-schema-shot">
          <img src={`${J}/templates.webp`} alt="The Templates tab showing a grid of predefined Journey templates by category and task count, with a Create from scratch option at the side" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p>
        That ended the back-and-forth with support. Admins could get a usable draft going on their own, and a few other Darwinbox modules later picked up the same three-way pattern.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Key design decisions -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="judgment">Key design decisions</SectionLabel>
      <h2>The core decision behind Playbooks.</h2>

      <div class="r-decisions">
        <DecisionBlock
          number="1"
          title="AI governance and the human-in-the-loop boundary"
          problem="Once the system could recognize a signal (a role change, a transfer, a new hire) and suggest a Playbook, we had to decide how much control to give the AI."
          options={[
            "AI assembles and activates the Playbook directly and the admin reviews after the fact.",
            "AI recommends and proposes steps. The admin reviews, edits and approves before anything is assigned.",
          ]}
          decision="AI proposes. The admin remains responsible for reviewing, editing and approving before anything ships to an employee."
          why="An HCM Playbook touches real employee experiences and compliance-sensitive steps. The admin needs to stay the accountable party. AI should remove the blank-page problem of starting from scratch, not remove the admin's judgment from a decision that affects real people."
          tradeoffs="Slower time to activation than a fully automated path. The interface also has to carry the extra weight of a clear review, edit and approve step rather than a single accept button."
          result="This human-in-the-loop model is the current design direction for Playbooks."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Craft -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="craft">Craft</SectionLabel>
      <h2>The work, in the order it was built.</h2>
      <p>
        For admins, Journeys is a configuration surface: choosing which steps, guidance and resources make up a process. For employees, it's one guided view of what to do and why, not a disconnected task list.
      </p>
<p class="r-sub-label">Flows: where it started</p>
      <figure class="r-video">
        <div class="r-video-frame">
          <video
            autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata"
            poster={`${FL}/flows-recording-clean-poster.jpg`}
            aria-label="A screen recording of the My Flows list, starting a request from a form, grouping flows by category, and opening one flow to see each stage and who has acted"
          >
            <source src={`${FL}/flows-recording-clean.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          <strong>My Flows:</strong> every request an employee is part of. Each one correct on its own, but nothing connects them into one experience.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Journeys: what was built on top</p>
            <ScreenGallery
        id="journeys-screens"
        screens={[
          {
            src: `${J}/journeys-list.webp`,
            name: "Journeys",
            alt: "The Journeys list in the admin settings, a table of Journeys with title, category, number of employees, progress, owner, origin and dates, with Templates and Journeys tabs above it",
            caption: "every Journey in one list, with its category, how many employees it reaches, progress and owner.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/templates.webp`,
            name: "Templates",
            alt: "The Templates tab showing a grid of predefined Journey templates by category and task count, with a Create from scratch option at the side",
            caption: "predefined Journeys to start from, grouped by category, or a blank one built from scratch.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/basic-details.webp`,
            name: "Basic details",
            alt: "Step one of the Create Journey flow with fields for title, category, description, event type, trigger event and user assignments, above a Help and Support section",
            caption: "the first of five steps. What the Journey is and when it applies: title, category, event and trigger, who it is assigned to and the help content attached to it.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/journey-initiation.webp`,
            name: "Initiation",
            alt: "Step two of the Create Journey flow showing initiator details with manual or auto trigger and a table of initiators, initiation steps and step content",
            caption: "the second step. Who starts the Journey and how, manual or automatic, and what content each step carries.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/approval-workflow.webp`,
            name: "Approval and workflow",
            alt: "Step three of the Create Journey flow showing an ordered table of approvers (self, L1 manager, HOD) with an initiation step for each, and a workflow selector below",
            caption: "the third step. The order of approvers and the workflow each step runs through.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/notifications-settings.webp`,
            name: "Notifications",
            alt: "Step four of the Create Journey flow showing a table that maps events to recipients, CC recipients and notification templates, with advanced toggles for confidentiality, auto revoke and hiding completed people",
            caption: "the fourth step. Who is told about which event, plus confidentiality, auto-revoke and visibility options.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/summary-preview.webp`,
            name: "Banner and preview",
            alt: "Step five of the Create Journey flow with banner upload and a gallery of banners on the left and a live preview of the employee-facing Journey on the right",
            caption: "the last step. Choose or upload a banner and preview the employee view before it goes out.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/journey-preview-employee.webp`,
            name: "Employee view",
            alt: "A Journey preview page for a first-time manager programme showing the overview, a week by week itinerary, reference documents and a help contact",
            caption: "what the employee sees: the Journey overview, an itinerary by stage, reference documents and someone to ask.",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </section>

    <!-- System: the evolution model, and where it's headed next -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="systems">System</SectionLabel>
      <h2>What comes next: the same model, made proactive.</h2>
      <p>
        Seen end to end, this is not three separate projects. It is one architecture getting progressively more proactive:
      </p>
      <ol class="r-evo-list">
        <li><strong>Flows:</strong> handled isolated transactional steps.</li>
        <li><strong>Journeys:</strong> connected those steps into a guided experience with a generative creation path.</li>
        <li><strong>Signals &amp; Playbooks:</strong> extends that same generative path from prompted creation to proactive system signals. This is the AI-native layer, still in build.</li>
      </ol>
      <FlowsMorph />

      <p class="r-evo-chain-label">The emerging interaction model for Playbooks:</p>
      <SignalChain />

      <p class="r-concept-label">Concept &middot; final design may change</p>
      <div class="r-concept-film">
        <FilmPlayer
          src={`${J}/playbooks-concept/playbooks-walkthrough-remotion.mp4`}
          poster={`${J}/playbooks-concept/playbooks-walkthrough-poster.jpg`}
          label="A walkthrough of the Signals and Playbooks concept: the live signal canvas, drilling into a Flight Risk signal, asking the AI direct questions about it, the AI recommending a Playbook with its reasoning steps shown, the admin initiating it, and then refining the live Playbook by asking for a change in plain English"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="r-concept-note">An internal concept exploration, not shipped product. Names and data shown are placeholders.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Collaboration -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="context">Collaboration</SectionLabel>
      <h2>How this got built.</h2>
      <p>
        Led as Senior Manager, UX Design, across Flows, Journeys and Playbooks. The core team was small: one junior designer and me on design, one PM, one person from the Center of Excellence, and three developers. I set the direction with product leadership, worked with the CoE partner on rollout, and partnered with the developers on the Flows engine and the AI recommendation layer.
      </p>
      <p>
        To be specific about where the line sits: the Journeys creation framework and the Playbooks human-in-the-loop decision are mine, originated and designed by me. Day-to-day execution on individual Journeys screens was split with the junior designer, with me reviewing and directing. Journeys is shipped and validated with clients through the roadshows below. Signals &amp; Playbooks is still a concept I'm driving, not yet built or validated with clients.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Outcome -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="impact">Outcome</SectionLabel>
      <h2>What shipped, and the adoption problem that came after.</h2>

      <div class="r-before-after">
        <div>
          <p class="r-ba-k">Before</p>
          <p class="r-ba-v">A disconnected task list. Admins rebuilding the same setup from scratch for every flow.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="r-ba-k">After</p>
          <p class="r-ba-v">An engaging, guided Journey. A usable draft in minutes, not a support call.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Journeys shipped and is actively used by clients: engaging workflows instead of a flat task list, with real adoption and less implementation overhead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shipping it wasn't the finish line. Once it was live, many clients didn't actually know how to use it, the same gap we saw with Time Management. We ran client roadshows there too: walking through what Journeys could do and turning the feature on with them, in the room. That's what made it stick instead of sitting unused.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <SectionLabel icon="judgment">Reflection</SectionLabel>
      <h2>What this work was really about.</h2>
      <p>
        The best part of this project was not drawing screens for Journeys. It was shifting the mindset from <em>"how do we help admins build things faster"</em> to <em>"why do they need to build them at all,"</em> and staying involved after launch to make sure the work actually drove results, not just shipped.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .r-case { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem); }
  .r-case-section > :is(p, h2, h3, blockquote, ul, ol, .r-case-label, .cn, .r-decisions, .r-sub-label) { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .r-case-section > :global(.fm), .r-case-section > :global(.sc) { max-width: var(--measure-head); }
  .r-case-section { margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .r-case-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-case-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 2.6vw, 1.875rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section p {
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section em { color: var(--color-ink); font-style: italic; }

  .r-decisions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .r-pullquote {
    max-width: var(--measure); margin: 1.75rem 0 1.75rem; padding: 0 0 0 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-ink); font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600; font-style: italic; letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-size: clamp(1.125rem, 2.1vw, 1.4375rem); line-height: 1.45; color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .r-schema-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .r-schema-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); align-items: start; max-width: none; }
  }
  .r-schema-shot {
    margin: 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 0.875rem;
    overflow: hidden; background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-schema-shot img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }

  .r-before-after {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1px; margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
    background: var(--color-border); border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 0.875rem; overflow: hidden; max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  @media (min-width: 560px) {
    .r-before-after { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
  .r-before-after > div { background: var(--color-bg); padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem; }
  .r-ba-k {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem !important;
  }
  .r-ba-v { font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 !important; }

  .r-schema-list { margin: 0 0 1.25rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .r-schema-list li {
    position: relative; padding-left: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .r-schema-list li::before { content: "–"; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-ink-3); }
  .r-schema-list strong { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .r-evo-chain-label { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }

  .r-concept-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem !important;
  }
  .r-concept-film { max-width: var(--measure-head); }
  .r-concept-film :global(.fp) { border-radius: 1.25rem; }
  .r-concept-note { font-size: 0.8125rem !important; color: var(--color-ink-3) !important; margin-top: 1rem !important; }

  .r-evo-list {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem; padding: 0; list-style: none;
    counter-reset: evo; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-evo-list li {
    counter-increment: evo; position: relative; padding-left: 2rem;
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .r-evo-list li::before {
    content: counter(evo); position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0.1rem;
    width: 1.375rem; height: 1.375rem; border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 22%, transparent);
    color: var(--color-ink-3); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  }
  .r-evo-list strong { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600; }


  .r-sub-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem !important;
  }
  .r-video { margin: 0; }
  .r-video-frame {
    overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--gal-r, 1.25rem); background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-video video {
    display: block; width: calc(100% + 10px); max-width: none; margin-left: -3px; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 1920 / 1188;
  }
  .r-video figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.9rem; max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .r-video figcaption strong { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
